cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Prime-abundant numbers enumerated without sieving, to 10^13"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: dataset
abstract: >-
  The prime-abundant numbers (n with rad(n) | sigma(n), OEIS A175200) are
  listed without sieving, by reading the defining condition backwards: every
  prime of n must divide sigma(q^a) for another prime power q^a of n, so no
  prime of n is free. This reaches 10^13 (85,695 terms) where the published
  b-file stops at 7,271,402,112 (10,000 terms), and extends the counts of
  prime-perfect numbers from 10^9 to 10^13. Includes three elementary theorems
  (unique factorisation into connected atoms; infinitude with an O(1)
  certificate; a closed count of the two-prime elements) and a set of negative
  results: six candidate invariants that do not predict |S(f)(x)|, measured
  across 30 local multiplicative functions.
